592186129371 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129371 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129371 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b59fa156-cc29-5899-ba12-deabeb536123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130085 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130085 #edb/ref 1061 27266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130085 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0882db2c-acfd-5afe-af78-e27777d7d1b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130085 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130086 #edb/ref 1061 27267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cad1b04-2ff0-5a9a-9273-5e82d2a22b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130087 #edb/ref 1061 27268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d716893c-ef21-57f8-8dc0-b5949c4ea6f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130088 #edb/ref 1061 27269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130088 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a3064a9-73a4-57bd-9ce0-df4e508faee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130088 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130093 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130093 #edb/ref 1061 27416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130093 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d93798a0-43ff-5a1e-a8fe-f7c3040adc4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1061 44390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "181d0ad9-f02a-5dcb-b1f6-c53db9361958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130404 #edb/ref 1064 "Evaluating double integrals by changing the variables to polar coordinates is often easier than using Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the disk with center $(0,0)$ and radius $2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/94/945f3881b5348fffb922d7b57fb42a6262eb03861fb5aa2411b947a16dcb3b59.png)\n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to plane polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $D$ is a disc of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ which has a simple polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\n             \n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130404 #edb/ref 1065 4433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130404 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "075c6789-1ae6-51fa-a4f4-f4a2d7ea1459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130404 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130406 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130406 #edb/ref 1065 4454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130406 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "04773792-9fe9-5ca8-8434-752f43db5fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130406 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275501 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02d7209c-7c17-506b-a4a6-86dde4f2d563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275788 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "22dc0a44-0404-58da-88dd-666cf5f8fdf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275810 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275810 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275810 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24f5786b-4836-55d8-ad56-141581c6fdc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276022 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276022 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3edc9dc7-a45f-5d0a-8152-3cecbb7db9af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276349 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60d9ad63-0c6e-5759-b321-954fef286724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186275810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b3c6d819-dfad-57d8-acd9-b4103349034b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4a72efd-94bd-57c9-9125-f124ca8a7f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278366 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17565734-926b-52e3-8121-3fbdaea7f5fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349073 #edb/ref 1080 "30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4509f43f-e1e1-5642-b552-7ac3e8a361a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349285 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b19110d-ad5e-5ffa-9e28-33a3151ac8f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349391 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349391 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349391 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "670fa60a-c544-5a09-9fe7-cd3fdc72ce28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349745 #edb/ref 1080 "40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349745 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349745 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "908f282c-0775-5149-a41c-32c4e538f86a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350865 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5f1e2bf0-e7fe-5f40-96b3-f24b49a5f589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06a556db-bf08-5756-8218-a6278b5b6e16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351516 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =20[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10{[{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =10(1-0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8c3056de2634d5315aebcbc978bf279eeb95da736ab6796c9d86dc39d9feadc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "548b0706-bd0c-5d06-92da-211a7b8ff882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394497 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394498 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eded17d8-4f57-5cb7-b32d-e7959dffa38a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394499 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394499 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394499 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8cbf1a51-29f1-5394-92b0-8fa0f03ed53d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394500 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394500 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394500 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27e50012-eea3-5128-88f0-eda7bc43ff75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394501 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc41e329-99f2-5f79-8b27-2fe659d98387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394502 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc17b81c-5c7b-56d4-81ee-2ee528fd1694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394503 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394503 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394503 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71b6433e-1c17-5544-8e0e-856c1b39a65e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f578d29d-0823-5f21-864f-baa92f133476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394504 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/fe/fe82880fec605db684a2b5cb0da5c88a38da5b4fb4faad1f80c4d5b0691fde21.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/9e/9e1693005c8930a7b2b8a3b47bc96f5441bf72ee6c5b67f3b8c97d87cfa2e34b.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394505 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "70a6a823-aaff-53a1-8971-3dd47ec005cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394506 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatornam